Run the following code using `bun`.

_You'll immediately see several print outs, after which you can exit it._

```ts
import { createServer } from 'node:http';
import { WebSocket, WebSocketServer } from 'ws';

const httpServer = createServer();

const wss = new WebSocketServer({
  server: httpServer,
  WebSocket,
});

wss.on('connection', ws => {
  // Following are two messages about to be sent, each with a slightly different way of calling the `ws.send` method:
  ws.send('foo', () => console.log('WS server had sent a "foo" message!'));
  ws.send('bar', {}, () => console.log('WS server had sent a "bar" message!'));
});

await new Promise<void>(resolve => httpServer.listen(4000, resolve));

const ws = new WebSocket('ws://localhost:4000');

ws.on('message', msg => {
  console.log(`WS client received a message: "${msg.toString()}"`);
});
```



### What is the expected behavior?

The print outs are expected to be:
```
WS server had sent a "foo" message!
WS server had sent a "bar" message!
WS client received a message: "foo"
WS client received a message: "bar"
```

### What do you see instead?

The actual print outs:
```
WS server had sent a "bar" message!
WS client received a message: "foo"
WS client received a message: "bar"
```

What's seen here is we're missing the server's first message log. The client nonetheless seems to have received both messages according to the prints. This means that the server, while it did send the message - it _**did not invoke the given completion callback**_ after it was done sending.

_I hope I didn't misunderstand the rational and concept in Bun's module replacements 🤞🏻🤔 I just recently started using Bun..._

It appears that Bun's replacement for the `ws`'s `WebSocket` class - `BunWebSocketMocked` - did not interpret the second argument given to `ws.send` in first message as being the completion callback. I suspect it is [expecting only 3 arguments exclusively](https://github.com/oven-sh/bun/blob/ae5b1280e1688bcaffa1ff7d476d6adc357c006e/src/js/thirdparty/ws.js#L729), while at that point in the repro snippet above it was given two. In any case, both calls to `ws.send` appearing in the repro snippet **should** be valid, according to the reference module `ws`'s docs of [WebSocket#send](https://github.com/websockets/ws/blob/master/doc/ws.md#websocketsenddata-options-callback:~:text=websocket.addEventListener().-,websocket.send(data%5B%2C%20options%5D%5B%2C%20callback%5D),-data%20%7BArray%7CNumber) which say the middle _"options"_ argument may be skipped and instead be supplied the callback.

### Additional information

My debugging journey to this had started due to my usage of [`graphql-ws`](https://github.com/enisdenjo/graphql-ws)'s GraphQL over WebSockets Server whose internal code is performing calls to `WebSocket#send` exactly in the first manner that was shown here.

As can be [seen in `graphql-ws`'s code](https://github.com/enisdenjo/graphql-ws/blob/63881c3372a3564bf42040e3f572dd74e41b2e49/src/use/ws.ts#L138), it promisifies the send call, invoking it via the two-argument form which is unpleasant for Bun. When running such server with Bun, it would end up in a never-resolving-promise situation and choke up all consequent outgoing messages (only the first one would be sent out) - I'm afraid right now that's what would happen any time you'd use `graphql-ws` together with Bun.

