### What steps can reproduce the bug?

When `await import(path)` evaluates a module whose top-level code throws, the first import correctly rejects with the evaluation error. Subsequent `import()` calls of the **same path** (sequential, not concurrent) return a partially-initialized module record instead of re-throwing the original error.

This violates the ESM spec — once a module's `[[Status]]` becomes `evaluated` with an evaluation error, [`HostResolveImportedModule`](https://tc39.es/ecma262/multipage/ecmascript-language-scripts-and-modules.html#sec-cyclic-module-records) must surface that same error on every subsequent attempt to evaluate it. Node.js does this correctly; Bun does not.

#### Repro 1 — `var X = undefinedRef` shape (silent partial initialization)

This is the exact shape produced by Bun's own bundler when a `_default` reference dangles.

```js
// broken-var.js
var resources_default = ResourcesComponent;
export { resources_default as default };
```

```js
// repro-var.js
const path = "./broken-var.js";
for (let i = 1; i <= 3; i++) {
  console.log(`=== import ${i} ===`);
  try {
    const m = await import(path);
    console.log(`  succeeded; default: ${m.default}, keys: ${Object.keys(m)}`);
  } catch (e) {
    console.log(`  threw: ${e.message}`);
  }
}
```

```
$ bun run repro-var.js
=== import 1 ===
  threw: ResourcesComponent is not defined
=== import 2 ===
  succeeded; default: undefined, keys: default
=== import 3 ===
  succeeded; default: undefined, keys: default
```

#### Repro 2 — `const X = undefinedRef` shape (TDZ on second call)

```js
// broken.js
export const ok = 1;
var fromUndeclared = MissingSymbol;
export const ok2 = 2;
```

```js
// repro.js
const path = "./broken.js";
for (let i = 1; i <= 2; i++) {
  console.log(`=== import ${i} ===`);
  try {
    await import(path);
  } catch (e) { console.log("threw:", e.message); }
}
```

```
$ bun run repro.js
=== import 1 ===
threw: MissingSymbol is not defined
=== import 2 ===
threw: Cannot access 'ok2' before initialization.
```

The second import surfaces a different (downstream) error — the original `MissingSymbol` reference error is lost.

### What is the expected behavior?

Per ECMA-262, evaluating an already-errored module record should re-throw the **same** evaluation error every time. Node confirms:

```
$ node repro-var.js
=== import 1 ===
  threw: ResourcesComponent is not defined
=== import 2 ===
  threw: ResourcesComponent is not defined
=== import 3 ===
  threw: ResourcesComponent is not defined
```

### What do you see instead?

See repros above. Bun's loader lets a partially-initialized module record become observable to the second `await import()`.

### Real-world impact

This hides bundler/codegen bugs that emit dangling references. In my case, a framework's bundle had a `var X = undefinedRef;` line that should have failed every request — but only the **first** request after a fresh `bun run dev` failed. Subsequent requests got back `default: undefined`, which the framework's downstream code treated as "the module just doesn't have a default export, fall back to scanning named exports." End-to-end the symptom was "first hit always 500s, retries succeed" — looked like an HMR race, was actually the loader masking a real error.

Any tool that lazy-loads user modules via `import()` (test runners, dev servers, plugin loaders) is exposed to this — a real evaluation bug in the user's module gets surfaced on the first call, then silently swallowed.

### Additional information

- Repro is fully sequential — no concurrent `Promise.all` involved (distinct from #20489 / #20159 / #28637 which are about concurrent `import()` + top-level `await` TDZ races).
- Reproduces deterministically every run on Linux x64 / Bun 1.3.13.
- Both `var` and `const` styles in the failing module surface the bug — the symptoms differ (silent `undefined` vs TDZ error on a sibling) but the underlying loader behavior is the same.

